# ADGM Court Procedure Rules 2016 — admissions and set-off

Source: ADGM Court Procedure Rules 2016 ("ADGM CPR"), admissions
framework. Pinned URL in `adgm_cpr_admissions_source.yaml`. In force;
current.

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md): ADGM Court Procedure Rules
2016, © Abu Dhabi Global Market. Reproduced from the ADGM-published
PDF for academic / research purposes.

## Rule 42 — Admissions (verbatim)

Reproduced verbatim from the ADGM Court Procedure Rules 2016 (consolidated
30 November 2023):

42. Admissions

(1) Rule 109 deals with the procedure in relation to a notice to admit
facts.

(2) Where the only remedy which the claimant is seeking is the payment
of money, the defendant may admit, by giving notice in writing in
accordance with the relevant practice direction, liability to pay the
whole or part of a claim for a specified amount of money or, where the
amount is not specified, the whole amount or part only of that claim.

(3) Where the defendant makes an admission as mentioned in paragraph
(2), the claimant has the right to enter judgment for the amount of
money for which liability has been admitted.

(4) The court may allow a party to amend or withdraw any admission made
by him on such terms as it thinks just.

(5) Where the Court has entered judgment in relation to a part of a
claim that has been admitted and the defendant has filed a defence in
relation to part of the claim that has not been admitted, the Court
shall proceed to determine that part of the claim.

(6) Where the Court has entered judgment in relation to a part of a
claim that has been admitted and the defendant has not filed a defence
in relation to part of the claim that has not been admitted, the
claimant may apply for default judgment on that part of the claim.

The set-off arithmetic between admitted sums (under Rule 42(3)) and
counterclaim sums proven on the balance of probabilities sits alongside
Rule 42 by force of the ADGM Civil Evidence, Judgments, Enforcement and
Judicial Cooperation Regulations 2015. This module captures that
arithmetic; the "what was admitted" and "what counterclaim items were
proven" are human-judgment inputs.

This module captures the *arithmetic* of the admissions/set-off
composition. The "what was admitted" and "what counterclaim items were
proven" are human-judgment inputs.

## Encoding

```catala
declaration structure AdmittedItem:
  data item_no content integer
  data admitted_aed content decimal

declaration structure CounterclaimItem:
  data item_no content integer
  data proven_aed content decimal

declaration structure AdmissionsAward:
  data admissions_total_aed content decimal
  data counterclaim_total_aed content decimal
  ## Clamped at 0.00 — under the admissions framework the *claimant's*
  ## right to enter judgment is for the admitted sums net of any
  ## proven counterclaim set-off. If the counterclaim exceeds the
  ## admissions, the surplus is a separate matter for the defendant's
  ## counterclaim — it is NOT a negative payment to the claimant. The
  ## raw signed difference is exposed in `signed_net_aed` for
  ## downstream consumers that want it.
  data net_to_claimant_aed content decimal
  data signed_net_aed content decimal
  data counterclaim_exceeds_admissions content boolean
  data counterclaim_surplus_aed content decimal

declaration scope AdmissionsAndSetOff:
  input admitted_items content list of AdmittedItem
  input counterclaim_items content list of CounterclaimItem
  output award content AdmissionsAward

scope AdmissionsAndSetOff:
  definition award equals
    let admitted_total equals
      sum decimal of (map each a among admitted_items
                      to a.admitted_aed)
    in
    let counter_total equals
      sum decimal of (map each c among counterclaim_items
                      to c.proven_aed)
    in
    let signed_net equals admitted_total - counter_total in
    let net_clamped equals
      if signed_net > 0.00 then signed_net else 0.00
    in
    let surplus equals
      if signed_net < 0.00 then 0.00 - signed_net else 0.00
    in
    AdmissionsAward {
      -- admissions_total_aed: admitted_total
      -- counterclaim_total_aed: counter_total
      -- net_to_claimant_aed: net_clamped
      -- signed_net_aed: signed_net
      -- counterclaim_exceeds_admissions: signed_net < 0.00
      -- counterclaim_surplus_aed: surplus
    }
```

## Used in

trace-04 — ADGMCFI-2024-320 *Projeco v Ideacrate* (admissions on
individual contract items, set-off against counterclaim).

## Tests

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestAdmissionsNetPositive:
  composite scope AdmissionsAndSetOff
  output award content AdmissionsAward

scope TestAdmissionsNetPositive:
  definition award equals composite.award
  definition composite.admitted_items equals [
    AdmittedItem { -- item_no: 1 -- admitted_aed: 100000.00 };
    AdmittedItem { -- item_no: 2 -- admitted_aed: 50000.00 }
  ]
  definition composite.counterclaim_items equals [
    CounterclaimItem { -- item_no: 1 -- proven_aed: 30000.00 }
  ]
  assertion award.admissions_total_aed = 150000.00
  assertion award.counterclaim_total_aed = 30000.00
  assertion award.net_to_claimant_aed = 120000.00
  assertion award.counterclaim_exceeds_admissions = false

#[test] declaration scope TestCounterclaimSwallowsAdmissions:
  composite scope AdmissionsAndSetOff
  output award content AdmissionsAward

scope TestCounterclaimSwallowsAdmissions:
  definition award equals composite.award
  definition composite.admitted_items equals [
    AdmittedItem { -- item_no: 1 -- admitted_aed: 100000.00 }
  ]
  definition composite.counterclaim_items equals [
    CounterclaimItem { -- item_no: 1 -- proven_aed: 250000.00 }
  ]
  ## Clamped: net to claimant cannot be negative under the admissions
  ## framework. The surplus is the defendant's counterclaim issue, a
  ## separate matter from the claimant's right to judgment.
  assertion award.net_to_claimant_aed = 0.00
  assertion award.signed_net_aed = -150000.00
  assertion award.counterclaim_exceeds_admissions = true
  assertion award.counterclaim_surplus_aed = 150000.00
```
